\title{Saturation versus Dilution: Harmonic Weighting of Quadratic-Residue
Dials, the Finiteness of the Count-Dial Optimum, and a Reciprocity-Flip Erratum}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{25 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For an odd integer $N$ and a window $P$ of small primes, the \emph{quadratic-residue
dial} of $N$ records, for each $\ell \in P$, whether $N$ is a square modulo $\ell$.
Two scalar summaries are in common use: the \emph{count dial}
$C(B) = \#\{\ell \le B : N \text{ a residue mod } \ell\}$, which weights all primes
equally, and the \emph{harmonically weighted dial}
$W(B) = \sum_{\ell \le B,\, N \text{ a residue}} 1/\ell$. Empirically these behave in
opposite ways as $B$ grows: the count dial's explained variance against an arithmetic
target decays from $32.07\%$ at $B=400$ to $0\%$ at $B=10^5$, while the weighted dial
moves only from $47.31\%$ at $B=400$ to $47.86\%$ at $B=10^6$, the two weighted
covariates correlating at $0.999$. We prove that this dichotomy is a theorem about
linear covariates in an orthonormal signal model with a non-flat amplitude profile.
Our main results are: an exact factorisation
$\Rcount = \flatf(T)\cdot \Rw$ in which the loss of equal weighting is a purely
geometric \emph{flatness} factor, strictly below $1$ whenever the amplitude profile is
non-constant; two-sided saturation $1 - 1/n \le \Rw \le 1 - 1/(8n)$ for the harmonic
profile, uniformly in the ambient population; dilution
$\Rcount \le (1+\log n)^2/n \to 0$; and \emph{finiteness of the count-dial optimum},
the ambient-free score $H_n^2/n$ attaining a global maximum at a finite window, which
refutes the pre-registered scale-shift alternative. We further diagnose a reciprocity
artifact: the prime-bottom and composite-bottom dial forms differ by the twist
character $\tau(\ell,N) = (-1)^{\lfloor \ell/2\rfloor \lfloor N/2\rfloor}$, which is
$-1$ exactly when $\ell \equiv N \equiv 3 \pmod 4$. Since $\tau$ is an involution the
flip destroys no information, yet it can annihilate all linear signal; a four-row
instance sends a perfect correlation to exactly zero covariance. A previously
published weakness of composite-bottom rows is therefore a dial-form artifact, while
the corresponding primary null result stands.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} quadratic residues, Jacobi symbol, quadratic reciprocity,
harmonic weighting, Cauchy--Schwarz defect, explained variance, covariate design,
window optimisation.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Dials}

Let $N$ be an odd integer and let $P$ be a finite set of odd primes not dividing $N$.
The \emph{quadratic-residue dial} of $N$ over $P$ is the sign vector
$\bigl(\chi_\ell(N)\bigr)_{\ell \in P}$ with $\chi_\ell(N) = \leg{N}{\ell} \in
\{\pm1\}$, the Legendre symbol, equal to $+1$ exactly when $x^2 \equiv N \pmod \ell$
is soluble. Dials are cheap---each bit is a Jacobi symbol computation in
$O(\log^2)$ bit operations---and informative, reflecting the splitting behaviour of
$N$ in quadratic fields.

To use a dial as a covariate against a target $y(N)$ one must compress the sign
vector into a scalar. The two natural compressions are
\[
C(B) = \#\{\ell \le B : \chi_\ell(N) = +1\},
\qquad
W(B) = \sum_{\substack{\ell \le B \\ \chi_\ell(N) = +1}} \frac{1}{\ell},
\]
the \emph{count dial} (equal weights) and the \emph{harmonically weighted dial}.

