Two questions follow. Is the count dial's decay evidence that the informative window
has \emph{moved outwards}, or that equal weighting is simply the wrong summary? And
why is the harmonic weight the right one? The pre-registered alternative was the
scale shift; it was refuted on all four shifted-window candidates, both legs failing
with $R^2$ margins of $-0.132$ to $-0.160$ against the absolute bar. This paper
supplies the theory behind that verdict, and separately diagnoses a reciprocity
artifact affecting a set of previously published secondary conclusions.

\section{The orthonormal signal model}

Let $(e_i)_{i \in \iota}$ be an orthonormal family in a real inner-product space,
indexed by primes. Fix a finite \emph{population} $S$ and an \emph{amplitude profile}
$a : \iota \to \mathbb{R}$; the \emph{target} is $s = \sum_{i \in S} a_i e_i$. Here
$e_i$ is the direction in which the residue status at the $i$-th prime pushes the
target, the directions are independent across primes, and $a_i$ measures how hard
that prime pushes. A \emph{window} is a subset $T \subseteq S$, and a \emph{dial} is a
covariate $u_c = \sum_{i \in S} c_i e_i$ with $c$ supported in $T$.

\begin{definition}[explained variance]
For $u,s$ in the space, $R^2(u,s) = \langle u,s\rangle^2 / (\|u\|^2\|s\|^2)$: the
squared cosine of the angle between $u$ and $s$, i.e.\ the fraction of the variance
of $s$ explained by a least-squares fit on $u$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:norm}
For an orthonormal family and any coefficients $c$,
$\bigl\|\sum_{i\in S} c_ie_i\bigr\|^2 = \sum_{i \in S}c_i^2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Expand the inner product of the sum with itself and use
$\langle e_i,e_j\rangle = \delta_{ij}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$R^2$ in the orthonormal model]\label{thm:r2}
For coefficient vectors $c,a$ supported in $S$,
\[
R^2\Bigl(\sum_{i\in S} c_ie_i, \sum_{i \in S} a_ie_i\Bigr)
= \frac{\bigl(\sum_{i\in S}c_ia_i\bigr)^2}
       {\bigl(\sum_{i\in S}c_i^2\bigr)\bigl(\sum_{i\in S}a_i^2\bigr)} .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The numerator is $\bigl(\sum_i c_i a_i\bigr)^2$ by orthonormality; the denominator is
the product of the squared norms, evaluated by Lemma~\ref{lem:norm}.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}\label{def:dials}
For a window $T \subseteq S$,
\[
\Rcount(S,T,a) = \frac{\bigl(\sum_{i\in T}a_i\bigr)^2}{|T|\sum_{i\in S}a_i^2},
\qquad
\Rw(S,T,a) = \frac{\sum_{i \in T} a_i^2}{\sum_{i\in S}a_i^2}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[the dials realise these ratios]
Taking $c_i = \mathbf{1}[i \in T]$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:r2} yields $\Rcount(S,T,a)$;
taking $c_i = a_i\mathbf{1}[i\in T]$ yields $\Rw(S,T,a)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For the indicator choice $\sum_S c_ia_i = \sum_T a_i$ and $\sum_S c_i^2 = |T|$. For
the amplitude choice $\sum_S c_ia_i = \sum_T a_i^2 = \sum_S c_i^2$, and the ratio
collapses to $\sum_T a_i^2/\sum_S a_i^2$; the degenerate cases
$\sum_T a_i^2 = 0$ and $\sum_S a_i^2 = 0$ give $0$ on both sides.
\end{proof}

The identification is exact: the empirical count dial \emph{is} the equal-coefficient
covariate and the harmonically weighted dial \emph{is} the amplitude-coefficient
covariate, provided the arithmetic amplitude satisfies $a_\ell \asymp 1/\ell$. That
proviso---a prime's influence scaling like the density $1/\ell$ of its congruence
class---is the sole arithmetic input to the analytic theory.

\section{Weighted dominates count: the flatness factorisation}

\begin{theorem}[Cauchy--Schwarz domination]\label{thm:dom}
If $\sum_{i\in S}a_i^2 > 0$ then $\Rcount(S,T,a) \le \Rw(S,T,a)$ for every window $T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $T = \emptyset$ both sides vanish. Otherwise Cauchy--Schwarz gives
$\bigl(\sum_T a_i\bigr)^2 \le |T|\sum_T a_i^2$; divide by the common positive
denominator $|T|\sum_S a_i^2$.
\end{proof}

The measured pair $0.3207 \le 0.4731$ at $B = 400$ is an instance. The size of the
gap is explained by the following.

\begin{definition}[flatness]
For a nonempty window $T$ with $\sum_T a_i^2>0$,
$\flatf(T,a) = \bigl(\sum_{i\in T}a_i\bigr)^2 / \bigl(|T|\sum_{i\in T}a_i^2\bigr) \in [0,1]$.
It is scale-invariant and equals $1$ exactly for a constant profile.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[exact factorisation]\label{thm:flat}
For a nonempty window $T$ with $\sum_T a_i^2 > 0$ and $\sum_S a_i^2>0$,
\[
\Rcount(S,T,a) = \flatf(T,a)\cdot \Rw(S,T,a).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\displaystyle
\flatf(T,a)\,\Rw = \frac{(\sum_T a_i)^2}{|T|\sum_T a_i^2}\cdot
\frac{\sum_T a_i^2}{\sum_S a_i^2} = \frac{(\sum_T a_i)^2}{|T|\sum_S a_i^2} = \Rcount .$
\end{proof}

Thus the entire penalty for equal weighting is a purely geometric quantity depending
only on the shape of the profile inside the window: the choice of dial and the choice
of window decouple.

\begin{lemma}[Lagrange's identity]\label{lem:lagrange}
$\displaystyle \sum_{i\in T}\sum_{j\in T}(a_i-a_j)^2
= 2\Bigl(|T|\sum_{i\in T}a_i^2 - \bigl(\textstyle\sum_{i\in T}a_i\bigr)^2\Bigr).$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Expand $(a_i-a_j)^2 = a_i^2 - 2a_ia_j + a_j^2$ and sum: the first and third terms each
contribute $|T|\sum_T a^2$, the cross term $-2(\sum_T a)^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[strict domination]
If $T$ contains $i_0,j_0$ with $a_{i_0}\ne a_{j_0}$ and $\sum_S a_i^2 > 0$, then
$\Rcount(S,T,a) < \Rw(S,T,a)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:lagrange} the Cauchy--Schwarz defect $|T|\sum_T a^2 - (\sum_T a)^2$
is half the sum of all pairwise squared differences, hence at least
$(a_{i_0}-a_{j_0})^2 > 0$; so $\flatf(T,a)<1$. Also $\sum_T a^2 > 0$, so $\Rw > 0$, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:flat} gives strict inequality.
\end{proof}

A $1/\ell$ profile over primes in $[3,10^6]$ has flatness of order
$(\log\log)^2/\pi(B)$: the theory predicts the weighted dial wins by a margin growing
with $B$, and the sweep confirms it ($.3207$ vs $.4731$ at $B = 400$; $.0277$ vs
$.4786$ at $B = 10^6$).

\section{Saturation versus dilution}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:mono}
Fix $S$ with $\sum_S a_i^2>0$. Then $T\mapsto \Rw(S,T,a)$ is nonnegative, monotone
non-decreasing in $T$, at most $1$ for $T \subseteq S$, and exactly $1$ for $T = S$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[tail bound implies saturation]\label{thm:tail}
If $\sum_{i\in S\setminus T}a_i^2 \le \theta \sum_{i \in S}a_i^2$ then
$\Rw(S,T,a) \ge 1-\theta$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\sum_T a^2 = \sum_S a^2 - \sum_{S\setminus T}a^2 \ge (1-\theta)\sum_S a^2$.
\end{proof}

Index the primes by $i = 0,1,2,\dots$ and take the harmonic profile
$a_i = 1/(i+1)$, the model form of $a_\ell \asymp 1/\ell$; let
$S=\{0,\dots,N-1\}$ and $T=\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ with $1\le n\le N$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:harm}
For $n\ge1$, $\sum_{i=n}^{N-1}(i+1)^{-2}\le 1/n$, and
$1 \le \sum_{i=0}^{N-1}a_i^2 \le 2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\frac{1}{(i+1)^2}\le\frac{1}{i(i+1)} = \frac1i - \frac1{i+1}$ for $i \ge 1$
telescopes to $\frac1n-\frac1N\le\frac1n$. The total is at least the $i=0$ term, $1$,
and at most that term plus the tail from $1$, which is at most $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[saturation]\label{thm:sat}
For all $1\le n\le N$, $\;\Rw \ge 1 - 1/n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:harm} the tail mass is at most $1/n$ and the total is at least $1$,
so the tail is at most $\frac1n$ times the total; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:tail}.
\end{proof}

The bound is \emph{uniform in $N$}: a window of the first $n$ primes captures at least
$1-1/n$ of the explainable variance whatever the ambient cutoff. This is the
theoretical content of $\operatorname{corr}(W(10^6),W(400)) = 0.999$.

\begin{theorem}[matching upper bound]\label{thm:satup}
If $2n \le N$ then $\Rw \le 1 - 1/(8n)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each of the $n$ indices $i \in [n,2n)$ has $a_i^2 \ge 1/(4n^2)$, so the tail beyond
the window has mass at least $1/(4n)$. The total is at most $2$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:harm}, so the tail is at least $1/(8n)$ of the total.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $2n\le N$, $\;1 - 1/n \le \Rw \le 1 - 1/(8n)$: the saturation rate is
$\Theta(1/n)$, so window size is a \emph{tolerance} parameter, not a \emph{scale}
parameter. In particular, for every $N \ge 400$, $\Rw \ge 0.9975$ at $n = 400$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[dilution]\label{thm:dil}
For all $1\le n\le N$, $\;\Rcount \le (1+\log n)^2/n$, and this tends to $0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The numerator of $\Rcount$ is $H_n^2 \le (1+\log n)^2$ and the denominator is
$n\sum_{i<N}a_i^2 \ge n$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:harm}. Since $\log^k x/x \to 0$ for each
fixed $k$, expanding $(1+\log n)^2 = 1 + 2\log n + \log^2 n$ gives the limit.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[saturation versus dilution]\label{thm:both}
For every $\varepsilon>0$ there is $n_0\ge1$ such that for all $n \ge n_0$ and all
$N \ge n$,
\[
\Rw \ge 1-\varepsilon \qquad\text{and}\qquad \Rcount \le \varepsilon .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $n_0 = \max(n_1,n_2+1)$ with $n_1$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:dil} and
$n_2 > 1/\varepsilon$; then $1/n \le \varepsilon$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:sat} gives the
first claim.
\end{proof}

Same primes, same bits, same window: a covariate that explains almost everything or
almost nothing, decided entirely by the weights. Equal weighting buries the
informative small primes---the signal grows like $\log n$ while the normaliser grows
like $\sqrt n$.

\section{The window is stronger, not shifted}

\begin{definition}
The ambient-free count score is $\mathrm{score}(n) = H_n^2/n$, $n \ge 1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$\Rcount \le \mathrm{score}(n) \le (1+\log n)^2/n$ for $1 \le n \le N$, and
$\mathrm{score}(1)=1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[finiteness of the count-dial optimum]\label{thm:opt}
There is a finite $B^\ast \ge 1$ with $\mathrm{score}(n) \le \mathrm{score}(B^\ast)$
for all $n \ge 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\mathrm{score}(n)\to0$, so there is $m$ with $\mathrm{score}(n)<\mathrm{score}(1)$
for all $n\ge m$. On the finite set $\{1,\dots,\max(m,1)\}$ the score attains a
maximum at some $B^\ast$, and $\mathrm{score}(B^\ast)\ge\mathrm{score}(1)>
\mathrm{score}(n)$ for every larger $n$.
\end{proof}

The scale-shift alternative asserts that the optimal window runs off to infinity with
the cutoff. Theorem~\ref{thm:opt} says it cannot. Combined with the empirical sweep,
which locates the maximum at $B^\ast = 400$, the verdict is \emph{window stronger, not
shifted}, and the previously reported location is confirmed scale-independent.

\begin{proposition}
For the weighted dial, $n \le n'$ implies $\Rw(\cdot,n,\cdot)\le\Rw(\cdot,n',\cdot)$
(Proposition~\ref{prop:mono}), so the ``optimal window'' question is vacuous: the
optimum is the whole population and the content is entirely the $\Theta(1/n)$ rate at
which small windows approach it.
\end{proposition}

\section{The reciprocity twist and the dial-form artifact}

There are two implementations of the residue bit: the \emph{clean (prime-bottom)}
form $\ell \mapsto \leg{N}{\ell}$, and the \emph{composite-bottom} form
$\ell \mapsto \leg{\ell}{N}$, a Jacobi symbol. The second was used to produce a set of
previously published secondary conclusions and gave weak covariates.

\begin{definition}[twist character]
$\tau(a,b) = -1$ if $a \equiv 3$ and $b \equiv 3 \pmod 4$, and $+1$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$\tau$ is symmetric, $\tau(a,b)^2 = 1$, and for odd $a,b$,
$\tau(a,b) = (-1)^{\lfloor a/2\rfloor\lfloor b/2\rfloor}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For odd $a$, $\lfloor a/2\rfloor$ is odd iff $a \equiv 3\pmod 4$; the product of the
two floors is odd iff both arguments are $3 \bmod 4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[reciprocity as a dial twist]\label{thm:twist}
For distinct odd primes $p,q$, $\;\leg{q}{p} = \tau(p,q)\leg{p}{q}$. For odd
$\ell, n$, $\;\leg{\ell}{n} = \tau(\ell,n)\leg{n}{\ell}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Quadratic reciprocity for the Legendre symbol and its Jacobi extension, with the sign
$(-1)^{\frac{p-1}{2}\frac{q-1}{2}}$ rewritten via the previous lemma.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[sharp dichotomy]\label{cor:dich}
For odd coprime $\ell,n$: if $\ell\equiv n\equiv 3\pmod 4$ then
$\leg{\ell}{n} = -\leg{n}{\ell}$ (the flip is total, with no exceptions); otherwise
$\leg{\ell}{n} = \leg{n}{\ell}$ (the forms agree identically). Hence the forms flip
\emph{iff} both arguments are $3 \bmod 4$.
\end{corollary}

This is exactly what the empirical audit found: a conditional flip rate of $100\%$
across all $2680$ qualifying rows with zero violations, and---on a population where
the condition held for $52.3\%$ of the values of $N$---an unconditional rate of
$27.19\%$, matching the predicted density to the second decimal place. Over the four
odd residue pairs $(\ell,n) \bmod 4 \in \{1,3\}^2$ the twist is $-1$ on exactly one,
so the unbiased unconditional density is $1/4$; and for fixed $\ell \equiv 3 \pmod 4$
the twist has mean zero over $n \in \{1,3\} \bmod 4$.

\begin{theorem}[a twist annihilates a perfect correlation]\label{thm:art}
Let four rows have targets $t = (+1,+1,-1,-1)$ and a clean dial perfectly aligned with
the target, $\mathrm{clean}_i = t_i$. Let the twist pattern be $w = (+1,-1,+1,-1)$ and
$\mathrm{flip}_i = w_i\,\mathrm{clean}_i$. Then
$\sum_i \mathrm{clean}_i t_i = 4$ while $\sum_i \mathrm{flip}_i t_i = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The clean covariances are $1,1,1,1$; the flipped ones $1,-1,1,-1$.
\end{proof}

Because $\tau$ is an involution the flipped dial is pointwise recoverable from the
clean one: no information is destroyed, only \emph{linear} signal. Consequently the
published weakness of the composite-bottom rows ($R^2 = .0781$ / dispersion reduction
$14.22\%$, and $.0565$ / $9.07\%$) is a dial-form artifact rather than an arithmetic
finding. The diagnosis is confirmed on three fronts: the weakness reproduces under the
flipped forms in the present population ($.030/4.11\%$ and $.0456/5.46\%$); the clean
prime-bottom dial on the same population is strong ($.3728/34.45\%$); and the
mechanism localises exactly where the theory says it must.

\begin{proposition}[localisation]
If every $\ell \in P$ satisfies $\ell \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ and $N$ is odd, then
\[
\sum_{\ell\in P}\frac{\mathbf 1[\leg{\ell}{N}=1]}{\ell}
= \sum_{\ell\in P}\frac{\mathbf 1[\leg{N}{\ell}=1]}{\ell} :
\]
the two dial forms are the \emph{same} covariate. All discrepancy is carried by the
$\ell \equiv 3 \pmod 4$ part of the window.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Termwise by Corollary~\ref{cor:dich}: the flip condition fails at each such $\ell$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[complementarity]
If $\ell \equiv N \equiv 3\pmod 4$ and $\gcd(\ell,N)=1$ then
$\leg{\ell}{N} = 1 \iff \leg{N}{\ell}\ne 1$: the dials are complementary there.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}[what is not retracted]
The \emph{primary} conclusion of the earlier work was a null result about a different,
individual-factor dial. That null replicates here ($R^2 = .0019$, dispersion reduction
$0.09\%$, $z = 0.72$) and the twist mechanism is inapplicable to it, the dial already
being in prime-bottom form. A discrepancy between two earlier measurements of the same
nominal quantity ($.078$ versus $.32$) resolves as a form difference plus estimator
spread---the recomputed composite-bottom dial matches the recorded values on all $128$
rows, and the clean dial is strong in both populations ($34.45\%$ and $34.75\%$).
\end{remark}

\section{What the product dial can and cannot see}

\begin{theorem}[XOR law]
For an odd modulus $\ell$ and integers $a,b$ coprime to $\ell$,
$\leg{ab}{\ell} = 1 \iff \leg{a}{\ell} = \leg{b}{\ell}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The symbol is multiplicative in its top argument and takes values in $\{\pm1\}$ under
coprimality; a product of two signs is $+1$ iff they agree.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
The weighted product dial is
$W(N,P) = \sum_{\ell \in P}\mathbf 1[\leg{N}{\ell}=1]/\ell$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[factor blindness]
If $\leg{p}{\ell}\leg{q}{\ell} = \leg{p'}{\ell}\leg{q'}{\ell}$ for all $\ell \in P$
then $W(pq,P) = W(p'q',P)$. In particular, if $p,q$ are residues at every modulus of
$P$ while $p',q'$ are non-residues at every modulus, the two dials coincide: the $PP$
and $NN$ factorisation types are invisible.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[squares saturate]
If $m$ is coprime to every $\ell \in P$ then $W(m^2,P) = \sum_{\ell\in P}1/\ell$, the
maximum possible value.
\end{theorem}

These are hard information-theoretic ceilings: no reweighting makes a covariate of
this form separate configurations it provably identifies.

\section{Dispersion bookkeeping}

Results were reported in two currencies: the fraction of \emph{raw} conditional
dispersion removed and the fraction of the \emph{excess above Poisson} removed.

\begin{theorem}[reading algebra]
If a covariate removes an amount $\Delta \ne 0$ from a conditional dispersion $D>1$,
the raw reading is $\Delta/D$, the excess reading is $\Delta/(D-1)$, and their ratio
is $(D-1)/D$, independently of $\Delta$.
\end{theorem}

Inverting, a raw/excess pair $(r,e)$ implies a baseline dispersion
$D(r,e) = 1/(1-r/e)$.

\begin{proposition}
The count dial at $B=400$ reported $(r,e) = (33.43\%,42.06\%)$ and the weighted dial at
$B=10^6$ reported $(48.51\%,61.00\%)$; the implied baselines satisfy
$\bigl|D(0.3343,0.4206) - D(0.4851,0.6100)\bigr| < 0.03$, both being near $4.88$. The
two rows are consistent readings of one overdispersion.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}
On both readings the weighted dial leaves strictly less unexplained than the count
dial, and on the excess-above-Poisson reading the two residual shares lie in
$[0.39,0.58]$ (weighted $39.00\%$, count $57.94\%$); on the raw reading the weighted
dial leaves $51.49\%$ unexplained.
\end{proposition}

Consequently a previously published claim that ``$\ge 86\%$'' of the structure is new
shrinks: the residual non-residue structure is $39$--$58\%$ on the excess reading and
about $51\%$ for the weighted dial on the raw reading. The remainder is still overdispersed, so the population is not fully explained
by the harmonic dial either; but the honest figure is roughly half, not six sevenths.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Computing the dials.} Sieve the primes $\ell \le B$; for each $\ell \nmid N$
compute $\leg{N}{\ell}$ by the binary Jacobi algorithm (repeated removal of factors of
$2$ with the $\ell \bmod 8$ rule, plus reciprocity swaps) in $O(\log^2\ell)$ bit
operations; accumulate $C(B) \mathrel{+}= 1$ and $W(B)\mathrel{+}= 1/\ell$ on a $+1$
symbol. Total $O(\pi(B)\log^2 B)$ per value of $N$, plus a shared one-off sieve.

\paragraph{Locating the count-dial optimum.} Theorem~\ref{thm:opt} guarantees a finite
maximiser of $H_n^2/n$, and $\mathrm{score}(n)\le(1+\log n)^2/n$ makes the search
explicit: compute $m$ with $(1+\log m)^2/m < 1$, then scan $1 \le n \le m$. The score
is unimodal in practice, so ternary search costs $O(\log m)$ evaluations.

\paragraph{Auditing a dial for reciprocity flips.} For each pair $(N,\ell)$ compute
both symbols, record $\textsf{flip} = [\leg{\ell}{N}\ne\leg{N}{\ell}]$ and
$\textsf{cond} = [\ell\equiv3 \wedge N\equiv3 \pmod 4]$, and assert
$\textsf{flip} = \textsf{cond}$ on every coprime row. By Corollary~\ref{cor:dich} the
assertion must hold with zero violations; any violation is an implementation bug (a
classic one being a Jacobi routine called with an even bottom argument, where the
symbol is undefined).

\paragraph{Flatness diagnostics.} Given an empirical amplitude profile $\hat a$ on a
window $T$, the number $\flatf(T,\hat a)$ \emph{is} the multiplicative penalty for equal
weights (Theorem~\ref{thm:flat}). Near $1$ licenses counting; near $0$---as for a
$1/\ell$ profile---mandates weighting. It costs one pass and requires no target values.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\subsection{Established}
Equal-weight counting is dominated at every window by harmonic weighting, with loss
exactly a flatness factor. With $1/\ell$ amplitudes the weighted dial saturates at rate
$\Theta(1/n)$ uniformly in the ambient population---a window of $400$ explains at least
$99.75\%$ of what any window can---while the count dial decays as $(1+\log n)^2/n$. The
count dial's optimum is attained at a finite window, refuting the scale shift
structurally. The two dial forms differ by an involutive twist that is $-1$ precisely
on $\ell\equiv N\equiv3\pmod4$, which converts a published weakness into an erratum
while leaving the associated primary null intact. The product dial obeys an XOR law, is
blind to the $PP$/$NN$ distinction, and is maximal on squares.

\subsection{Limitations}
The analytic results are theorems about an orthonormal signal model with a prescribed
amplitude profile. The model's fidelity rests on two assumptions---approximate
independence of per-prime contributions, and $a_\ell \asymp 1/\ell$---both standard
heuristics for residue statistics, neither proved here from the arithmetic. The
constant $400$ is not derived; what is derived is that some finite optimum exists for
the count dial and that the weighted dial's residual error at window size $n$ lies
between $1/(8n)$ and $1/n$, which is what makes $400$ adequate rather than magic. The
reciprocity results, by contrast, are unconditional arithmetic. No
complexity-theoretic consequence is claimed.

\subsection{Future directions}
\textbf{Optimal weight profiles beyond $1/\ell$.} The factorisation
$\Rcount = \flatf(T)\Rw$ identifies the loss with a purely geometric quantity, so the
search for the best dial is a search for the weight vector maximising a Rayleigh
quotient, not for a better cutoff. The maximiser is the amplitude profile itself; the
question is how much is lost by an arithmetically computable approximation---$1/\ell$
versus $\log\ell/\ell$ versus $1/(\ell\log\ell)$.

\textbf{The twist as a character sum.} The flipped dial equals the clean dial times
$\chi_4(\ell)\chi_4(N)$, so its covariance with any target splits into a
$\chi_4$-twisted character sum; the observed near-zero correlation should be a
cancellation theorem. Mean-zero over residues is available; the missing step is a
quantitative bound on the twisted sum over a window of primes, a
P\'olya--Vinogradov-type estimate.

\textbf{Saturation rate as a spectral gap.} The quantity $1-\Rw(n)$ is the normalised
tail mass of the amplitude profile---the spectral tail of the signal operator---so
``saturation by $400$'' says that operator has rapidly decaying spectrum. Both
directions of the rate are proved in the model, so the rate is $\Theta(1/n)$ there;
extending this to the arithmetic operator would make ``which window suffices'' a
computable function of the desired tolerance for any target.

\textbf{Beyond linear covariates.} Since the twist is an involution, the information
lost to a linear model is recoverable by any model conditioning on $N \bmod 4$.
Quantifying the gap between the best linear dial and the best $\bmod\ 4$-conditioned
dial would turn the erratum into a positive result about interaction terms.

\subsection{Methodological remarks}
Pre-registration prevented an easy story: the scale-shift alternative was fixed before
the data were generated and refuted on all four candidates. Adversarial checking ran in
both directions---several first-draft claims were retracted on verification, one
proposed rescue of the weak dial form (dropping the modulus $\ell = 2$) was tested and
rejected empirically, and a small-sample smoke test that spuriously supported the shift
hypothesis was marked non-evidentiary rather than quietly reported. Finally, errata are
cheap when the mechanism is a theorem: once the difference between two dial forms is
identified as multiplication by an explicit $\pm1$ character, ``was the weak result
real?'' reduces to a two-line computation and an audit assertion that either holds on
every row or reveals a bug.

\end{document}

